Z||000000||fierowner||09-14-1999||10:13 PM||||My car dies on the Interstate; I got no response from the Gas Pedal when i stepped on it. My dad at first claimed it to be the Fuel  Pump. Since its in the gas tank, we looked for other alternatives. He looked over the relays and all that and told me to go try it.  I turn it over and i hear the pump kick on.  YES! Problem solved, right? ERRR wrong.  I sit there and crank and crank and crank until i have carpal tunnel, and my dad tries to get the spark wire to spark against the engine (to see if we are getting any spark) we didnt get a spark.  We go to the Auto parts store and get an igniton coil.  We put it in, and it still does not start.  Its rare if we even get a spark!   Please help! ||||reg||8
Z||000001||Cooter||09-14-1999||11:21 PM||||Ignition Module?????||||reg||3
Z||000002||AL||09-15-1999||12:25 AM||||take your distributor out, and go get it checked. some times they ground them slves out, i know i had the same problem. no spark nothing, so i took the distributor out, had it checked and it was no good. i bought a new one and presto it worked.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||09-15-1999||12:32 AM||||You can install a new ignition module (pick up coil) for $12 and 1/2 hour.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||09-15-1999||08:44 AM||||If your car died straight away, you've got no spark, and it hasn't so much as sputtered in a weak attempt to start, that sure sounds like an ignition module to me.  They're less than $20 at AutoZone, and as Tom pointed out, it's an easy job.  You probably won't even get dirty!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-15-1999||11:39 AM||||You know, I can't believe the prices of parts over there... I paid US$65 for a new ignition module here in Holland!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Anyway - sure sounds like a broken ignition module to me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dale||09-15-1999||01:41 PM||||Cliff you need to find someone to send you some parts, OUCH!  But then again I have the same problem on the Island.||||reg||10
Z||000007||mshill||09-15-1999||03:35 PM||||I guess I am not shopping in the right places either.  I paid $52 for an ignition module and $11 for the pickup coil for my V6.  Just replaced both last Saturday.<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000008||fierowner||09-15-1999||05:19 PM||||We just put a new Ignition Module in a few months ago; I'll run it by my dad, see what he wants to do, but I've just about had it with hot starters not starting, leaky sunroofs, and now no spark; This car is for sale once we get it fixed.||||reg||13
Z||000009||fierowner||09-15-1999||10:16 PM||||Ignition Module isnt the prob. The rotor doesnt spin when I crank it.  My dad suggests a timing belt may be the problem...any thoughts?||||reg||4
Z||000010||Cooter||09-15-1999||10:28 PM||||Broken timing chain (v6), stripped cam gear (4cyl) or a broken camshaft. Any of these usually means disaster in the area of piston/valve contact.||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||09-15-1999||10:34 PM||||That don't sound good! It the timing chain or gears went out, then the cam won't spin either. You could pull a valve cover off and crank the engine to see if the valves and/or rocker arms are moving. If not...bingo!||||reg||1
Z||000012||fierowner||09-23-1999||09:37 PM||||Okay; It was a stripped Camshaft timing gear.  It needs to be removed. The Haynes Manual says it has to be removed with a hydraulic press! i dont want to take that engine out! Any other way??  84 SE 4cyl.  thanks alot||||reg||3
Z||000013||ChadMan||09-24-1999||09:33 AM||||Ouch!  Fierowner, I feel your pain, when you love your car but you come to a crossroads where you're like, "Damn, should I put another $700-$1000 in this thing!?!?"<P>Cooter is right - you might have valvetrain damage too.  Y'know, I bet Ed Parks would put a stock 2.8 V6 in your 84 for less than a local shop would charge to install a rebuilt 2.5.  Food for thought...||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||09-24-1999||09:43 AM||||there is a tool set to change the gear on the car. I don't know how well it works.<P>You need to get the oil pan off and remove the crap that fell in there from the gear.<P>The normal method is to pull the cam. This can be done with the motor in the car. It requires dropping the back of the cradle a bit.<P>The above tool is for the 4 cyl..... I didn't think the V6 was pressed?????<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-24-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000015||Carrolles||09-24-1999||10:47 AM||||I don't believe the v-6 valve train allows the valves to strike the pistons if the cam stops turning.<P>The only way that I know of to remove the cam is out the front which requires taking the engine out or at least disconnection it from the trans and tilting it up so much that you might as well remove it.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fierowner||09-24-1999||11:25 PM||||My dad worked on it today while I was at school, and he cut it off or something like that.  He ordered a new one, and now the only question is how to put the new one on...He suggested sand it down a little and lube it up and slide her on....any thoughts on this one?||||reg||1
